# Explore the Combinatorics of Everything

A fixed-length book is a word of length $L$ over an alphabet of $A$ symbols. There are exactly $A^L$ such books. Start by feeling how quickly that elementary exponent overwhelms physical intuition.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

[Learn more about the rule of product](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_product).

## Two geometries on one library

Hamming distance counts coordinates that disagree. Every distinct pair is at distance at least $1$, so a ball of radius $1/2$ contains one book: the metric topology is discrete. Yet joining books at distance $1$ produces a connected graph.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the connectivity proof</summary>

For books $x$ and $y$, list their mismatching positions and replace them one at a time. This gives a graph path of length $d_H(x,y)$. Conversely, each edge repairs at most one mismatch, so no shorter path exists. Topologically, however, every singleton is clopen; any space with at least two books is separated by one singleton and its complement.

</details>

## Shells around a book

Exactly $ inom{L}{k}(A-1)^k$ books lie at distance $k$ from a fixed book. Choosing the positions supplies the binomial coefficient, and choosing new symbols supplies the power.

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the sphere-count identity</summary>

Summing all shells gives

$$\sum_{k=0}^L  inom{L}{k}(A-1)^k=(1+A-1)^L=A^L,$$

so the shells partition the whole library.

</details>

## The shortage of short names

A decoder with $N$ programs has at most $N$ distinct outputs. Consequently at least $A^L-N$ books are undescribed whenever $N\le A^L$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the binary probability bound</summary>

A binary library has $2^L$ books. A budget of $2^{L-c}$ descriptions covers at most the fraction $2^{L-c}/2^L=2^{-c}$. Thus at least $1-2^{-c}$ of uniformly sampled books are incompressible relative to that decoder and budget.

</details>

## Experiment with random books

Typical pairs of random books are far apart. Over $A$ symbols, each coordinate disagrees with probability $1-1/A$, so expected Hamming distance is $L(1-1/A)$.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal assumptions and edge cases</summary>

If $L=0$ there is one empty book. If $A=1$ there is one book of every length. If $A=0<L$ the library is empty. The topological nonconnectedness theorem concerns the genuine case $A\ge2$ and $L>0$. Exact complexity of an individual book depends on the chosen decoder; the invariant result is the counting bound for each fixed decoder.

</details>

For background, see introductions to [Hamming distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamming_distance), [zero-dimensional spaces](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero-dimensional_space), and [Kolmogorov complexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolmogorov_complexity).
